Attribute Effect Macro equivalent <code>Tag{"name"}</code> Sets the tag for system loggers that support tags. none <p>On Android, messages written to logcat are tagged with the short program name. <code>Tag</code> overrides it per message:</p> Tagging a message<pre><code>#include &lt;ng-log/experimental/log.h&gt;

ngx::Log(ngx::Warning | ngx::Tag{"network"}, "Connection lost");
</code></pre> <p>The tag must stay valid until the call returns. Other platforms ignore it.</p>

Attribute Effect Macro equivalent <code>Every{n}</code> Logs the first and every <code>n</code>-th occurrence. <code>LOG_EVERY_N(severity, n)</code> <code>First{n}</code> Logs the first <code>n</code> occurrences. <code>LOG_FIRST_N(severity, n)</code> <code>Every{duration}</code> Logs at most once per duration. <code>LOG_EVERY_T(severity, seconds)</code> <p>The argument of <code>Every</code> selects how it limits the rate: an integer counts occurrences and a duration limits the time between messages. The counter type of <code>Every</code> and <code>First</code> is deduced from <code>n</code>. Any standard integer type is accepted:</p> Choosing the counter type<pre><code>#include &lt;ng-log/experimental/log.h&gt;

ngx::Log(ngx::Every{1000}, "msg");               // counts using int
ngx::Log(ngx::Every{std::size_t{1000}}, "msg");  // counts using std::size_t
</code></pre> <p>Counting never overflows, even if <code>n</code> is the maximum of the counter type. The occurrence count below wraps around at the limit of the type instead.</p> <p>A period is a <code>std::chrono</code> duration of any representation. A number without a unit, such as a <code>double</code> of seconds, does not compile, since it is neither a count nor a period:</p> Rate limiting by time<pre><code>#include &lt;ng-log/experimental/log.h&gt;

using namespace std::chrono_literals;

ngx::Log(ngx::Every{500ms}, "msg");
ngx::Log(ngx::Every{0.5s}, "msg");
ngx::Log(ngx::Every{0.5}, "msg");  // does not compile
</code></pre> <p>Durations beyond the range of <code>std::chrono::nanoseconds</code> saturate. Like <code>LOG_EVERY_T</code>, the first message is logged only once the steady clock exceeds the duration since its epoch.</p> <p>The <code>Occurrence</code> argument formats how often the call site occurred, like <code>LOG_OCCURRENCES</code> and <code>nglog::COUNTER</code>, in the counter type of <code>Every</code> or <code>First</code> counting occurrences. Like <code>LOG_EVERY_N</code>, <code>Every</code> counts the occurrences and decides which to log separately, so messages logged concurrently can report counts other than those of the occurrences logged. A period does not count occurrences, so using <code>Occurrence</code> without a count does not compile:</p> Logging the occurrence count<pre><code>#include &lt;ng-log/experimental/log.h&gt;

ngx::Log(ngx::Every{100}, "Got the {}th cookie", ngx::Occurrence);
</code></pre>

Macros<pre><code>LOG(INFO) &lt;&lt; "x=" &lt;&lt; x;

LOG(DFATAL) &lt;&lt; "msg";



LOG_AT_LEVEL(s) &lt;&lt; "msg";
</code></pre> Functions<pre><code>ngx::Log("x={}", x);

ngx::Log(ngx::Error |
             ngx::FatalInDebug,
         "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::Severity{s}, "msg");
</code></pre>

Macros<pre><code>LOG_IF(ERROR, c) &lt;&lt; "msg";



LOG_EVERY_N(INFO, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";

LOG_EVERY_N(INFO, 10)
    &lt;&lt; "n=" &lt;&lt; LOG_OCCURRENCES;

LOG_IF_EVERY_N(INFO, c, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";



LOG_FIRST_N(INFO, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";

LOG_EVERY_T(INFO, 0.5) &lt;&lt; "msg";
</code></pre> Functions<pre><code>if (c) {
  ngx::Log(ngx::Error, "msg");
}

ngx::Log(ngx::Every{10}, "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::Every{10}, "n={}",
         ngx::Occurrence);

if (c) {
  ngx::Log(ngx::Every{10}, "msg");
}

ngx::Log(ngx::First{10}, "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::Every{500ms}, "msg");
</code></pre>

Macros<pre><code>DLOG(WARNING) &lt;&lt; "msg";


DLOG_IF(WARNING, c) &lt;&lt; "msg";




DLOG_EVERY_N(INFO, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";


DLOG_FIRST_N(INFO, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";


DLOG_EVERY_T(INFO, 1) &lt;&lt; "msg";


DLOG_IF_EVERY_N(INFO, c, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";




if (DCHECK_IS_ON()) {
  // ...
}
</code></pre> Functions<pre><code>ngx::Log(ngx::Warning | ngx::Debug,
         "msg");

if (c) {
  ngx::Log(ngx::Warning | ngx::Debug,
           "msg");
}

ngx::Log(ngx::Debug | ngx::Every{10},
         "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::Debug | ngx::First{10},
         "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::Debug | ngx::Every{1s},
         "msg");

if (c) {
  ngx::Log(
      ngx::Debug | ngx::Every{10},
      "msg");
}

if constexpr (ngx::IsDebugEnabled()) {
  // ...
}
</code></pre>

Macros<pre><code>VLOG(2) &lt;&lt; "msg";

VLOG_IF(2, c) &lt;&lt; "msg";



VLOG_EVERY_N(2, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";



VLOG_IF_EVERY_N(2, c, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";





DVLOG(2) &lt;&lt; "msg";


if (VLOG_IS_ON(2)) {
  // ...
}
</code></pre> Functions<pre><code>ngx::Log(ngx::Verbose{2}, "msg");

if (c) {
  ngx::Log(ngx::Verbose{2}, "msg");
}

ngx::Log(ngx::Verbose{2} |
             ngx::Every{10},
         "msg");

if (c) {
  ngx::Log(ngx::Verbose{2} |
               ngx::Every{10},
           "msg");
}

ngx::Log(ngx::Verbose{2} | ngx::Debug,
         "msg");

if (ngx::IsEnabled(ngx::Verbose{2})) {
  // ...
}
</code></pre>

Macros<pre><code>PLOG(ERROR) &lt;&lt; "open " &lt;&lt; name;


PLOG_IF(ERROR, c) &lt;&lt; "open";




PLOG_EVERY_N(ERROR, 10) &lt;&lt; "open";
</code></pre> Functions<pre><code>ngx::Log(ngx::Error |
         ngx::Errno)("open {}", name);

if (c) {
  ngx::Log(ngx::Error |
           ngx::Errno)("open");
}

ngx::Log(ngx::Error | ngx::Errno |
         ngx::Every{10})("open");
</code></pre>

Macros<pre><code>SYSLOG(INFO) &lt;&lt; "msg";

SYSLOG_IF(INFO, c) &lt;&lt; "msg";



SYSLOG_EVERY_N(INFO, 10) &lt;&lt; "msg";



LOG_TO_SINK(&amp;sink, INFO) &lt;&lt; "msg";

LOG_TO_SINK_BUT_NOT_TO_LOGFILE(&amp;sink,
                               INFO)
    &lt;&lt; "msg";

LOG_TO_STRING(INFO, &amp;text) &lt;&lt; "msg";

LOG_STRING(INFO, &amp;texts) &lt;&lt; "msg";
</code></pre> Functions<pre><code>ngx::Log(ngx::Syslog, "msg");

if (c) {
  ngx::Log(ngx::Syslog, "msg");
}

ngx::Log(ngx::Syslog |
             ngx::Every{10},
         "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::ToSink{&amp;sink}, "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::ToSink{&amp;sink} |
             ngx::SkipLogFile,
         "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::ToString{&amp;text}, "msg");

ngx::Log(ngx::ToVector{&amp;texts},
         "msg");
</code></pre>
